# taskpump.conf — TaskPump configuration
#
# Copy to `taskpump.conf` at the root of the repo TaskPump drives.
#
# ── Discovery ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TaskPump walks up from $PWD looking for `taskpump.conf`, stopping at the
# enclosing git worktree root. The search starts from the *caller's* directory,
# never from where the tools are installed — so in a repo with worktrees, each
# worktree gets its own configuration and its own ledger, and running the
# primary checkout's copy of a tool from inside a worktree still operates on the
# worktree. Set TASKPUMP_CONFIG to name a file explicitly and skip the walk.
#
# A missing config file is normal: nearly every key below has a default baked
# into the tool that reads it. The few with none say so in their own entry, and
# two of those — TASKPUMP_IMAGE and TASKPUMP_LOCAL_AGENT_CMD — are required for a
# real launch and abort loudly rather than guess.
#
# ── Precedence ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   environment  >  taskpump.conf  >  tool default
#
# Legacy ARACHNE_* names are honored during the transition. An ARACHNE_X in the
# ENVIRONMENT sets TASKPUMP_X and still outranks this file; every TASKPUMP_X set
# here is mirrored to ARACHNE_X, so a config written in canonical names reaches a
# tool that still reads the legacy spelling. Where both spellings are in the
# environment, TASKPUMP_X wins. Three limits, all in docs/CONFIG.md §2: an
# ARACHNE_* name written IN THIS FILE never bridges (the snapshot that makes
# environment outrank file is taken before the file is sourced); ARACHNE_CONFIG
# names no config file and silently moves the conf anchor; and TP_* names are not
# bridged at all — they are the pump-to-runner handoff's own spelling.
#
# ── Syntax ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file is sourced by bash with allexport on, so it is `KEY=value` per line.
# Quote any value containing spaces:
#     TASKPUMP_NOTIFY_CMD='logger -t taskpump'
# The notifier here is logger and not anything desktop because this line is
# about the quoting and gets copied by people who are not reading it for
# notifications: a configured notifier reads the message on stdin, which logger
# does and an argv-style desktop notifier does not, and it has to work headless,
# which is where a long run lives. The key's own entry below has the desktop
# wrapper and what it needs.
#
# ── Reading this file ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every key is commented out and annotated with the default the tool falls back
# to. Uncomment only what you need to change. Where two spellings resolve to one
# setting, the name LISTED is the one to set and any other spelling is named in
# the description — usually the canonical TASKPUMP_ name, but not always: where
# the code reads only the other one, the working spelling is what is listed and
# the description says so in capitals. docs/CONFIG.md names each tool that reads
# a key, which is the question behind most of those cases.
#
# Anything a consumer never sets — values the pump hands its runner, or the
# runner hands the container — is listed at the very bottom under "Runtime
# contract", for reading rather than setting.

# ── Discovery ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_CONFIG=          # this file's path; skips the upward walk. Pointing it
#                           #   at a missing file is an error, never a fallback.

# ── Ledger (tp task) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A RELATIVE path in this file is anchored to the workspace the file describes
# (a discovered conf's own directory; the caller's worktree root under an
# explicit TASKPUMP_CONFIG) — never to the caller's $PWD. See docs/CONFIG.md
# "Relative paths in the conf".
# TASKPUMP_TASKS_DIR=       # directory of task files (default: <repo root>/$TASKPUMP_LEDGER_PROBE)
# TASKPUMP_LEDGER_PROBE=    # path, relative to a candidate workspace, whose presence
#                           #   means "this workspace owns a ledger" — how a worktree's
#                           #   own ledger is told from the primary's (default: tasks).
#                           #   TASKPUMP_TASKS_SUBDIR is its fallback spelling and a
#                           #   TRAP: tp-pump, tp-monitor and tp-dag-render honour it,
#                           #   tp-task does not (it defaults LEDGER_PROBE before
#                           #   resolution runs), so setting it moves three tools'
#                           #   ledger and not the ledger CLI's. See docs/CONFIG.md
#                           #   §3.1. Set this key, never that one.
# TASKPUMP_LEDGER_REPO=     # the ledger CHECKOUT the pump refreshes and pushes, and the
#                           #   root the container resolves its ledger paths against
#                           #   (default: <repo root>/ops). Read by tp-pump and the
#                           #   entrypoint, not by tp-task — the ledger DIRECTORY is
#                           #   TASKPUMP_TASKS_DIR above.
# TASKPUMP_CODE_REPO=       # repo whose commits the heartbeat productivity check
#                           #   measures — the code, not the ledger (default: the
#                           #   resolved repo root)
# TASKPUMP_TASK_OUT=        # JSON "next task" surface file
#                           #   (default: <parent of tasks dir>/.next-task)
# TASKPUMP_TASK_EXT=        # the task-file extension tp-monitor, tp-dag-render and the
#                           #   container entrypoint SCAN for (default: .md). tp-task
#                           #   hardcodes .md and never reads this, so setting it points
#                           #   the readers at files the writer does not create
#                           #   (TASKPUMP_TASK_FILE_EXT is accepted in the container only)
# TASKPUMP_ID_PATTERN=      # regex `create` validates a new id against
#                           #   (default: ^T[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$)
# TASKPUMP_TASK_PUSH=       # 1 = git push after each state commit (default: 0;
#                           #   TASKPUMP_PUSH accepted)
# TASKPUMP_PUSH_RETRIES=    # push attempts before giving up (default: 6)
# TASKPUMP_TASK_NOCOMMIT=   # 1 = never commit state — fixture and dry-run mode (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_TASK_DEBUG=      # 1 = trace resolution to stderr (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_CLAIM_STALE_HOURS=  # age at which scrub relabels a claim that stopped
#                           #   beating (default: 24)
# TASKPUMP_FAILURE_LIMIT=   # consecutive unproductive iterations before scrub marks
#                           #   a task stuck (default: 3)
# TASKPUMP_TURN_BUDGET_DEFAULT=  # `claim` budget when --turns is omitted (default: 50)
# TASKPUMP_LOCK_WAIT=       # seconds to wait for the state lock (default: 120)
# TASKPUMP_LOCK_NAME=       # the state lockfile's name, created in the ledger's git
#                           #   root and flock-shared by inode across containers —
#                           #   every concurrent agent must resolve the same name.
#                           #   Removed when the verb that took it exits
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-task.lock)
# TASKPUMP_COMMITTER_NAME=  # identity for ledger commits (default: tp-task)
# TASKPUMP_COMMITTER_EMAIL= # (default: task@taskpump.local)
# TASKPUMP_PROG_NAME=       # the name the CLI calls itself in diagnostics
#                           #   (default: tp-task)
# TASKPUMP_TASK=            # path to the task CLI other tools invoke; set it when the
#                           #   tools are not co-installed (default: <install>/libexec/tp-task)

# ── Id grammar (tp monitor, tp dag-render) ───────────────────────────────────
# How an id is split into phase and sub-task for grouping and layout. These are
# the parsing half of TASKPUMP_ID_PATTERN above; keep the three consistent.
# TASKPUMP_PHASE_SIGIL=     # letter a phase token starts with (default: T)
# TASKPUMP_PHASE_SEPARATOR= # what ends the phase within an id (default: .)

# ── Pump scheduling (tp pump) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_WORKSPACE_ROOT=  # the workspace the pump drives: the default image-build
#                           #   context, the repo phase branches and agent worktrees
#                           #   are created in, the state-dir default. (default:
#                           #   derived from the config anchor — a discovered conf's
#                           #   directory, else the caller's worktree root — never
#                           #   from a vendored install's own checkout, issue #32.
#                           #   Set it to pin containers/CI; a missing directory is
#                           #   a loud error.) Not pump-only: `tp task` resolves its
#                           #   ledger inside the same pin, so one pinned shell gets
#                           #   one answer from both tools (issue #45).
# TASKPUMP_JOBS=            # concurrent worktree pool cap (default: 4). This is the
#                           #   key the pump reads; TASKPUMP_PUMP_JOBS is the systemd
#                           #   unit's name for the same number, passed as --jobs.
# TASKPUMP_JOBS_FALLBACK=   # cap apl_read_cap falls back to for a caller that passes
#                           #   none (default: 6). The pump ALWAYS passes its own JOBS,
#                           #   so this cannot decide a pump run — its live consumer is
#                           #   tp-disk-watchdog, restoring a cap it has no other record of
# TASKPUMP_POOL_CAP_FILE=   # live-editable pool cap, re-read each tick
#                           #   (default: <state dir>/.taskpump-pool-cap)
# TASKPUMP_TICK=            # seconds between supervisor ticks (default: 30)
# TASKPUMP_STAGGER=         # seconds between launches within one tick (default: 3)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_TASKS_DIR=  # tasks dir for the pump alone, independent of the ledger
#                           #   checkout (default: TASKPUMP_TASKS_DIR, else
#                           #   <workspace root>/$TASKPUMP_LEDGER_PROBE — the same
#                           #   derivation tp task and tp monitor use)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_OPS_DIR=    # ledger checkout the pump refreshes and pushes
#                           #   (default: TASKPUMP_LEDGER_REPO, else <repo root>/ops)
# TASKPUMP_WORKTREES_DIR=   # where agent worktrees live — under two incompatible
#                           #   contracts. tp-pump and tp-cleanup read it as a PATH
#                           #   (default: <repo root>/.worktrees); tp-monitor reads it as
#                           #   a bare NAME and builds <repo root>/<name> (default:
#                           #   .worktrees). An absolute value hands the monitor
#                           #   <repo root>//abs/path; a bare name leaves the other two
#                           #   with a cwd-relative path (it is not anchored like the
#                           #   ledger keys). Prefer TASKPUMP_PUMP_WORKTREES_DIR for the
#                           #   path and leave this one to the monitor.
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_WORKTREES_DIR=  # pump-only override of the above
# TASKPUMP_BRANCH_PREFIX=   # prefix for per-phase branches (default: feat/;
#                           #   TASKPUMP_PUMP_BRANCH_PREFIX overrides for the pump)
# TASKPUMP_BASE=            # base ref new branches are cut from (default: main;
#                           #   TASKPUMP_PUMP_BASE overrides for the pump)
# TASKPUMP_RESUME_MAX=      # no-progress resumes of a stalled claim before escalating
#                           #   (default: 3; TASKPUMP_PUMP_RESUME_MAX overrides)
# TASKPUMP_STALL_EXIT_TICKS=  # consecutive deadlocked ticks before exiting 3
#                           #   (default: 3; TASKPUMP_PUMP_STALL_EXIT_TICKS overrides)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_QUARANTINE_FILE=  # append-only record of merges the integration trunk
#                           #   refused: one `<unit> <sha> <reason>` line per failure,
#                           #   read once to paste into the graduation PR body. NOT an
#                           #   exclusion list — a phase named here is still dispatched
#                           #   (default: <state dir>/.auto-trunk-quarantine)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_MONITOR=    # 0 = do not hand the terminal to the monitor after
#                           #   --detach (default: 1)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_DEBUG=      # 1 = verbose supervisor tracing (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_NO_LAUNCH=  # 1 = plan only, never start a container (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_NO_GH=      # 1 = skip all gh CLI calls (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_NOTIFY_CMD=      # command that reads a notification on stdin. Unset falls
#                           #   back to notify-send when present, else log only. Set to
#                           #   `true` to silence. A desktop popup needs a wrapper and a
#                           #   session bus to deliver to:
#                           #   TASKPUMP_NOTIFY_CMD='xargs -0 notify-send -u low TaskPump'
#                           #   (without -0, xargs splits the message at whitespace into
#                           #   extra positionals notify-send rejects). Headless —
#                           #   enable-linger, ssh — there is no bus and every notice
#                           #   fails, so there use
#                           #   TASKPUMP_NOTIFY_CMD='logger -t taskpump'
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_PROG_NAME=  # the name the pump answers to as itself — diagnostics,
#                           #   the plan header, the notify title, the transient
#                           #   systemd unit (default: tp-pump)

# ── Run state and file names (tp pump) ───────────────────────────────────────
# One knob moves every dotfile the PUMP drops; each name is still individually
# overridable.
# TASKPUMP_STATE_DIR=       # where the run's dotfiles live (default: the repo root).
#                           #   It moves the writer, not the readers: tp-monitor derives
#                           #   the pump state file from the PRIMARY CHECKOUT's root and
#                           #   tp-dag-render from its own repo root — neither reads this
#                           #   key — so a moved state dir also needs an absolute
#                           #   TASKPUMP_PUMP_STATE_FILE, which both sides honour.
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_STATE_FILE= # absolute path of the supervisor state file, overriding
#                           #   both the state dir and the name below
#                           #   (default: <state dir>/<TASKPUMP_PUMP_STATE_NAME>)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_STATE_NAME= # its filename within the state dir
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-pump.state)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_LOG=        # supervisor log path (default: <state dir>/.taskpump-pump.log)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_LOG_NAME=  # per-worktree agent log. The monitor and the cleanup
#                           #   sweeper find a live agent by this filename, so renaming
#                           #   it makes running agents invisible to them
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-agent.log)
# TASKPUMP_BRIEF_OUT_NAME=  # rendered brief written into each worktree
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-phase-brief.md)
# TASKPUMP_GOAL_NOTE_NAME=  # goal note written into each worktree (default: .taskpump-goal.md)
# TASKPUMP_RESUME_NOTE_NAME=  # resume preamble written into a resumed worktree
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-resume.md)
# TASKPUMP_HOOK_MARK_FILE=  # fingerprint of the last pre-tick hook output, kept so
#                           #   a persistent condition notifies once rather than
#                           #   every tick (default: <state dir>/.taskpump-fsguard.notified;
#                           #   TASKPUMP_FSGUARD_MARK_FILE is its older spelling)

# ── Project-shaped behavior (tp pump) ────────────────────────────────────────
# The keys that make the pump fit a language and a toolchain. None of them ship
# with a toolchain baked in; a consumer names its own commands here.
# TASKPUMP_BUILD_GATE=      # command that must pass before work is integrated. UNSET IS
#                           #   NOT "no gate": with --integration-trunk the pump then runs
#                           #   `cargo check --workspace`, then ./smoke_test.sh if the
#                           #   worktree has one. On a non-Rust project that can only
#                           #   fail, and every merge is reset --hard, quarantined
#                           #   "build red", and the lead task flipped to needs-review.
#                           #   Set it — to your own command, or to `true` to integrate
#                           #   ungated. (TASKPUMP_PUMP_BUILD_CMD overrides for the
#                           #   merge queue; a configured command REPLACES the default,
#                           #   it does not run alongside it.)
# TASKPUMP_VERIFY_CMDS=     # newline-separated commands a task must leave green,
#                           #   quoted into the brief (default: empty — the brief's
#                           #   verify sections are dropped until a consumer sets it)
# TASKPUMP_PROJECT_BRIEF=   # one paragraph pointing an agent at the project's own
#                           #   contributor docs (default: a generic "read CLAUDE.md,
#                           #   CONTRIBUTING.md, or equivalent" paragraph)
# TASKPUMP_BRIEF_TEMPLATE=  # the parameterized brief a launched agent is handed.
#                           #   Resolution: this key (or TASKPUMP_PHASE_BRIEF_TEMPLATE),
#                           #   then <install>/templates/phase-drain-brief.md. Two rungs,
#                           #   no probing: a template the pump was never told about is
#                           #   not configuration, whatever directory it sits in.
# TASKPUMP_TASK_BRIEF_TEMPLATE=
#                           # the brief a --grain task container is handed. A DIFFERENT
#                           #   template, not a reworded phase brief: the phase brief's
#                           #   working method is the in-context `next --phase` loop, and
#                           #   an agent running that at task grain would claim the very
#                           #   siblings the pump just dispatched elsewhere.
#                           #   Resolution: this key, then
#                           #   <install>/templates/task-brief.md
# TASKPUMP_RESUME_TEMPLATE= # resume preamble a stalled task's agent gets ahead of that
#                           #   brief; resolves the same two ways, ending at
#                           #   <install>/templates/resume-note.md
# TASKPUMP_TASK_CLI=        # how an agent invokes the ledger CLI from inside its
#                           #   worktree — the spelling briefs quote at the agent
#                           #   (default: tp task)
# TASKPUMP_SUBMODULE_PROBE= # a worktree-relative path that proves the ledger submodule
#                           #   is populated, letting the pump skip its per-worktree
#                           #   `git submodule update --init --recursive`. NO default:
#                           #   unset, the (idempotent) init always runs. A probe that
#                           #   exists for the wrong reason skips the init some OTHER
#                           #   submodule still needs — silently — so set it only to a
#                           #   path that proves everything you need populated.
# TASKPUMP_RECLAIM_CMD=     # how to reclaim build output from a finished workspace, so
#                           #   a multi-day run's disk footprint stays bounded — run with
#                           #   the workspace as its cwd, e.g. 'cargo clean'
#                           #   (default: empty = the reclaim pass and the tp-cleanup
#                           #   --targets sweep are no-ops that log themselves unconfigured)
# TASKPUMP_RECLAIM_DIR=     # the build-output dir that marks a workspace as worth
#                           #   reclaiming, and the one tp-cleanup --status measures
#                           #   (default: target). A PROBE, not the thing deleted: set it
#                           #   to whatever your build writes (build, dist,
#                           #   node_modules), or to empty to run TASKPUMP_RECLAIM_CMD in
#                           #   every idle workspace. Read by tp-cleanup only — the
#                           #   pump's per-tick pass still hardcodes target/.
# TASKPUMP_DISK_RECLAIM=    # 0 = switch off the pump's per-tick reclaim of FINISHED
#                           #   phases' build output (default: 1). Not disk-pressure
#                           #   conditional despite the name — the pass runs every tick,
#                           #   before the feed gate, so a paused run keeps freeing space.
#                           #   The pressure-driven one is TASKPUMP_PANIC_RECLAIM below.
# TASKPUMP_GATES=           # ordered, newline-separated list of gate COMMAND LINES —
#                           #   each entry's first word must be an executable path; a
#                           #   non-executable entry is skipped with a warning.
#                           #   (default: gates/claude-token-fresh, `<usage bin> --gate`,
#                           #   gates/disk-low — each included only while its own
#                           #   enable flag is 1; gates/net-health joins first when
#                           #   TASKPUMP_HEALTH_GATE=1 opts it in)
# TASKPUMP_PRE_TICK_HOOKS=  # newline-separated hook command lines run at the start of
#                           #   each tick, same executable-path rule as gates
#                           #   (default: <install>/hooks/gitignore-repair and
#                           #   <install>/hooks/fs-guard)
# TASKPUMP_MANIFEST=        # OPT-IN consumer-supplied v1 parallel-run manifest — a
#                           #   TSV of name<TAB>branch<TAB>brief<TAB>task rows that
#                           #   tp-monitor/tp-cleanup consult for launch-time labels
#                           #   (default: none — TaskPump does not generate the file;
#                           #   the ledger's live claim is the canonical mapping, and
#                           #   a configured path that does not exist is an error)
# TASKPUMP_MANIFEST_SUBPATH=  # the same file named relative to the repo root
#                           #   (default: none)

# ── Integration trunk (opt-in; off unless a trunk flag is passed) ────────────
# TASKPUMP_TRUNK=           # integration trunk branch name (default: auto/trunk;
#                           #   TASKPUMP_PUMP_TRUNK overrides)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_TRUNK_PUSH= # 1 = push the trunk after each merge (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_TRUNK_LOCK_FILE=  # flock guarding the merge queue
#                           #   (default: <state dir>/.auto-trunk.lock)
# TASKPUMP_TRUNK_LOCK_WAIT= # seconds to wait for the trunk lock (default: 300)
# TASKPUMP_INTEGRATION=     # base the trunk is cut from (default: main;
#                           #   TASKPUMP_PUMP_INTEGRATION_BASE overrides)
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_INTEGRATE_DRYRUN=  # 1 = rehearse the merge queue without merging
#                           #   (default: 0)

# ── Hooks (hooks/fs-guard, hooks/gitignore-repair) ───────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_FS_GUARD_ALLOWLIST=  # extended regex of paths allowed to be dirty in the
#                           #   primary checkout; anything else dirty means a container
#                           #   wrote where it should not have. (default:
#                           #   ^(\.worktrees/|ops$|ops/|\.auto-trunk\.lock$|\.auto-trunk-quarantine$))
# TASKPUMP_WORKTREES_IGNORE_LINE=  # the bare ignore line the repair hook strips out of
#                           #   .gitignore (default: .worktrees/)

# ── Gate: net-health (gates/net-health) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Host-hardware policy, not a general one — it looks for the kernel signatures of
# a specific WiFi firmware wedge. Shipped but OFF by default; a consumer whose
# hardware needs it opts in.
# TASKPUMP_HEALTH_GATE=     # 1 puts the gate at the head of the default chain
#                           #   (default under tp-pump: 0 — the gate is not consulted;
#                           #   gates/net-health run by hand defaults to 1). A net-health
#                           #   entry in a custom TASKPUMP_GATES still needs this key.
# TASKPUMP_HEALTH_WINDOW=   # how far back to look, seconds (default: 120)
# TASKPUMP_HEALTH_PROBE_CMD=  # command whose output is searched (default: empty =
#                           #   `journalctl -k -b 0 --since '<window> sec ago'`)
# TASKPUMP_HEALTH_SIGNATURES=  # extended-regex alternation of failure strings
#                           #   (default: Failed to alloc SKB|Firmware reported general
#                           #   error|Timeout on response for query command)

# ── Gate: claude-token-fresh (gates/claude-token-fresh) ──────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_TOKEN_GATE=      # 0 disables the gate (default: 1)
# TASKPUMP_TOKEN_MARGIN_S=  # seconds of TTL headroom the gate wants (default: 600)
# TASKPUMP_CREDENTIALS=     # host credentials file: the freshness gate checks its
#                           #   expiry, the usage gate pulls its OAuth token
#                           #   (default: $HOME/.claude/.credentials.json; under the pump,
#                           #   <TASKPUMP_AGENT_HOME>/.credentials.json — derived only
#                           #   when unset, so a value set here is never clobbered).
#                           #   What the container copies is TASKPUMP_AGENT_HOME.

# ── Gate: disk-low (gates/disk-low) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_DISK_GATE=       # 0 disables the low-disk feed gate for a HAND-RUN gate
#                           #   (default: 1). Under tp-pump it decides nothing: the pump
#                           #   hardcodes 1 and exports it over your value, so only
#                           #   --no-disk-gate (or dropping the gate from TASKPUMP_GATES)
#                           #   takes it out of the chain.
# TASKPUMP_DISK_WATCHDOG=   # path to the watchdog the gate consults
#                           #   (default: <install>/libexec/tp-disk-watchdog)
# The floor itself is TASKPUMP_DISK_PAUSE_GB, under the watchdog section below.

# ── Gate: claude-usage (gates/claude-usage) ──────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_GATE=      # 0 disables the usage gate for a HAND-RUN gate (default: 1).
#                           #   Under tp-pump it decides nothing, exactly as
#                           #   TASKPUMP_DISK_GATE above: use --no-usage-gate.
# TASKPUMP_USAGE=           # path to the usage-gate binary
#                           #   (default: <install>/gates/claude-usage)
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_CEILING=   # utilization percent at which to pause launching (default: 95)
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_ENDPOINT=  # OAuth usage endpoint URL
#                           #   (default: https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage)
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_CACHE=     # cached usage payload path
#                           #   (default: /tmp/claude-plan-usage.json)
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_TTL=       # cache freshness, seconds (default: 60)
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_HTTP_TIMEOUT=  # curl timeout, seconds (default: 8)
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_RESET_FILE=  # reset-backstop file. The gate's own standalone
#                           #   default is <repo root>/.claude-usage-reset; under the
#                           #   pump it is resolved to <state dir>/.taskpump-usage-reset
#                           #   and handed down, which is the default that counts in
#                           #   a driven run.
# TASKPUMP_USAGE_DEBUG=     # 1 = trace the probe to stderr (default: 0)

# ── Runner selection and launch (runners/claude-docker/runner.sh) ────────────
# TASKPUMP_RUNNER=          # path to the runner EXECUTABLE the pump launches agents
#                           #   with — not a runner name
#                           #   (default: <install>/runners/claude-docker/runner.sh)
# TASKPUMP_DOCKER=          # container-runtime binary (default: docker)
# TASKPUMP_IMAGE=           # container image name. NO DEFAULT: a real run aborts
#                           #   loudly when unset — a wrong silent image is worse
#                           #   than a loud missing one. --dry-run needs no image.
# TASKPUMP_IMAGE_BUILD=     # command that builds the image before the first launch.
#                           #   The tree's only +set-tested key: UNSET runs the default
#                           #   `docker build -t <image> <workspace>`; SET BUT EMPTY
#                           #   skips the build, which is what a runner pulling a
#                           #   prebuilt image wants. Since this file is sourced with
#                           #   allexport, uncommenting the line above with no value IS
#                           #   the second case, not the first.
# TASKPUMP_ENTRYPOINT=      # in-image entrypoint path (default: /entrypoint.sh —
#                           #   where the image contract bakes the shipped runner's
#                           #   entrypoint.sh; see docs/RUNNERS.md)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_PREFIX=    # container-name prefix, INCLUDING the trailing dash —
#                           #   liveness enumeration matches on it, so it must be
#                           #   distinctive on the host (default: tp-agent-).
#                           #   TASKPUMP_AGENT_CONTAINER_PREFIX is a fallback spelling
#                           #   ONLY tp-monitor honours: set it alone and the monitor
#                           #   watches one prefix while the pump launches, enumerates
#                           #   and reclaims under another.
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_MODEL=     # model alias forwarded to each agent (default: opus)
# TASKPUMP_MAX_TURNS=       # turn cap for a phase-drain session (default: 600)
# TASKPUMP_TASK_MAX_TURNS=  # turn cap for a --grain task session, which claims ONE task
#                           #   and exits rather than draining a sub-tree for hours
#                           #   (default: 120)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_HOME=      # host agent home copied into the container
#                           #   (default: $HOME/.claude)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_CONFIG=    # host agent config file (default: $HOME/.claude.json)

# ── Runner, container side (runners/claude-docker/entrypoint.sh) ─────────────
# TASKPUMP_PRE_FLIGHT=      # an executable INSIDE the container, run as root before the
#                           #   session — toolchain setup, smoke tests, anything
#                           #   project-shaped. Non-zero exit aborts the launch with 75.
#                           #   (default: none)
# TASKPUMP_CONTAINER_USER=  # user the session runs as (default: dev)
# TASKPUMP_CONTAINER_HOME=  # its home (default: /home/<container user>; the shipped
#                           #   hooks/agent-preflight defaults the same key to a
#                           #   hardcoded /home/dev, so changing the user and relying on
#                           #   that hook lands the merged settings in a home the session
#                           #   never reads — runners/claude-docker/preflight-example.sh
#                           #   follows the entrypoint and does not)
# TASKPUMP_WORKSPACE_TASK_CLI=  # the ledger CLI the container invokes: absolute, or
#                           #   workspace-relative, or a bare command found on PATH
#                           #   (default: tp — startup fails loudly if it is absent)
# TASKPUMP_SAFETY_TURNS=    # turn budget for the entrypoint's safety-net claim (default: 3)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_WALL_TIMEOUT_S=  # wall-clock cap on a session; firing exits 124
#                           #   (default: unset = no cap)
# TASKPUMP_CRED_REFRESH_INTERVAL_S=  # how often the container re-copies the host's
#                           #   access token (default: 300)
# TASKPUMP_HOST_CRED_MOUNT= # where the host credential dir is mounted
#                           #   (default: /tmp/claude-home)
# TASKPUMP_HOST_CONFIG_MOUNT=  # where the host agent config is mounted
#                           #   (default: /tmp/claude-home-json/.claude.json)
# TASKPUMP_RO_PROBE_FILE=   # filename used to prove the primary mount is read-only
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-rotest)
# TASKPUMP_INSTALL_MOUNT=   # where a TaskPump install is mounted in the image; its bin/
#                           #   is prepended to PATH (default: /opt/taskpump). The shipped
#                           #   runner deliberately does not forward it, so setting it
#                           #   HERE is inert — it is for an image, or a caller that
#                           #   mounts an install and passes the variable itself.

# ── Runner: local processes (runners/local/runner.sh) ────────────────────────
# An alternative runner that starts an agent as a bare process instead of a
# container. It never sources lib/config.sh, so THIS FILE DOES NOT REACH IT when
# it is run by hand (`runner.sh list`, `runner.sh stop`) — only the environment
# does. Under a pump launch it inherits the pump's loaded environment and these
# values arrive normally.
# TASKPUMP_LOCAL_AGENT_CMD= # REQUIRED for `launch`: the shell command that runs one
#                           #   agent session, run through bash -c in the workspace.
#                           #   NO default — a plausible-but-wrong guess at your agent
#                           #   would fail far from here (TP_LOCAL_AGENT_CMD accepted)
# TASKPUMP_LOCAL_REGISTRY=  # the `<name> <pgid> <workspace>` registry that gives a bare
#                           #   process the name→process mapping a container runtime has
#                           #   for free (default: <state dir>/.taskpump-local-agents
#                           #   when TASKPUMP_STATE_DIR is set, else
#                           #   ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/taskpump/local-agents;
#                           #   TP_LOCAL_REGISTRY accepted)
# TASKPUMP_LOCAL_STOP_GRACE_S=  # seconds between the TERM to the process group and the
#                           #   KILL (default: 10; TP_LOCAL_STOP_GRACE_S accepted)

# ── Monitor (tp monitor) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_BIN=     # path to the monitor the pump hands off to
#                           #   (default: <install>/libexec/tp-monitor)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_REPO_ROOT=  # checkout whose worktrees to report on (default: the
#                           #   caller's repo root)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_MAIN_ROOT=  # primary checkout, used for cache keys and notes
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_INTERVAL=  # redraw period in seconds; fractional ok (default: 1).
#                           #   Watching is the tty default, so this is an ordinary
#                           #   `tp monitor`'s refresh rate (--watch is a deprecated
#                           #   alias for it; --glance is the one-frame opt-out)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_TITLE=   # title-line text (default: TaskPump)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_COLS=    # terminal width override (default: the real width)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_LINES=   # terminal height override (default: the real height)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_LOG=     # redraw-instrumentation log (default: empty = off)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_TAIL_LINES=  # agent-log lines per session in the compact feed
#                           #   (default: 2)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_LOG_SCAN=  # log lines scanned per session to build that feed (default: 200)
# TASKPUMP_AGENT_LOG_FILTER=  # jq program pulling feed lines out of an agent log
#                           #   (default: empty = the built-in filter)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_TERM=    # the GUI terminal BINARY the `o` key spawns to open a task
#                           #   file — not a $TERM override (default: wezterm)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_TERM_ARGS=  # its argv template; %CLASS% and %CWD% expand
#                           #   (default: start --always-new-process --class %CLASS%
#                           #   --cwd %CWD% --)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_TASK_CLASS=  # window class for that spawned viewer, so a compositor
#                           #   can rule on it (default: taskpump-task)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_OPEN_CMD=  # replaces the whole spawn; the file path is appended
#                           #   (default: empty = use the terminal keys above)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_USAGE_WINDOWS=  # comma-separated `label=datefmt` list of usage gauges
#                           #   (default: 5h=+%H:%M,7d=+%a %H:%M)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_DISK=    # 0 = hide the disk gauge (default: 1)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_DISK_PAUSE_GB=  # the gauge's pause threshold (default: 10)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_DISK_PANIC_GB=  # the gauge's panic threshold (default: 5)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_NOTES_DIR=  # directory of session notes
#                           #   (default: <main root>/<notes dirname>)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_NOTES_DIRNAME=  # that directory's name
#                           #   (default: .taskpump-monitor-notes)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_NOTES_FILE=  # a single notes file to tail, overriding the resolved
#                           #   path (default: empty = resolve from the dir)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_NOTES_TAIL=  # note lines shown in the panel (default: 6)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_NOTES_COLW=  # max note width in the right column (default: 60)
# The monitor redraws often and must not re-probe the world each frame, so every
# probe is cached. Cache paths derive from the base unless set individually.
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_CACHE_BASE=  # cache path prefix (default: $TMPDIR/taskpump-monitor)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_SESS_CACHE=  # session probe cache file
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_SESS_TTL=  # session probe cache seconds (default: 2)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_PUMP_CACHE=  # pump-state cache file
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_PUMP_TTL=  # pump-state cache seconds (default: 8)
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_DISK_CACHE=  # disk probe cache file
# TASKPUMP_MONITOR_DISK_TTL=  # disk probe cache seconds (default: 60)

# ── DAG rendering (tp dag-render) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_DAG_BIN=         # path to the renderer the monitor invokes
#                           #   (default: <install>/libexec/tp-dag-render)
# TASKPUMP_DAG_REPO_ROOT=   # repo root the renderer resolves the ledger against
# TASKPUMP_DAG_LAYOUT=      # the layout program (default: <install>/lib/dag-layout.awk)
# TASKPUMP_AWK=             # the gawk binary; the layout engine needs GNU awk, not mawk
#                           #   (default: gawk)

# ── Cleanup (tp cleanup) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_CLEANUP_REPO_ROOT=  # repo whose worktrees get swept. DEFAULT: THE INSTALL
#                           #   ROOT (the parent of the invoked script's dir), not the
#                           #   caller's repo — unset, a sweep run from your project
#                           #   measures TaskPump's own checkout and reports success.
# TASKPUMP_RECLAIM_PRIMARY= # 1 = same as --include-primary (default: 0)
# TASKPUMP_EXTRA_BUSY_DIRS= # newline-separated workspace roots --targets must also
#                           #   skip, so a workspace that is mid-compile keeps its build
#                           #   output. A listed primary root is skipped even under
#                           #   --include-primary. (default: empty)
#                           #   The bare EXTRA_BUSY_DIRS is the legacy spelling and still
#                           #   works; this one wins when both are set, and the skip line
#                           #   names whichever answered.
# TASKPUMP_STOP_GRACE_SEC=  # container stop grace period, seconds (default: 30)

# ── Agent watchdog (tp agent-watchdog) ───────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_WATCHDOG_POLL=   # seconds between sweeps (default: 120)
# TASKPUMP_STUCK_THRESHOLD_MIN=  # agent-log staleness cutoff, minutes (default: 15)
# TASKPUMP_STARTUP_GRACE_SEC=  # with --auto-exit, ignore a zero-agent state for this
#                           #   many seconds after start (default: 120)
# TASKPUMP_EMPTY_GRACE_CHECKS=  # with --auto-exit, exit after this many consecutive
#                           #   zero-agent checks (default: 3)

# ── Disk watchdog (tp disk-watchdog) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# TASKPUMP_DISK_REPO_ROOT=  # repo the watchdog measures and reclaims within. Same
#                           #   default as TASKPUMP_CLEANUP_REPO_ROOT above, and the same
#                           #   hazard: it is the INSTALL root unless you set it.
# TASKPUMP_DISK_MOUNT=      # mount to measure (default: /)
# TASKPUMP_DISK_PROBE=      # command reporting free space
#                           #   (default: df --output=avail -BG '<mount>')
# TASKPUMP_DISK_POLL=       # seconds between checks (default: 30)
# TASKPUMP_DISK_PAUSE_GB=   # free below this → pool cap 0; also the disk-low gate's
#                           #   floor (default: 10)
# TASKPUMP_DISK_PANIC_GB=   # free below this → reclaim build dirs and prune (default: 5)
# TASKPUMP_DISK_RECOVER_GB= # free above this → restore the original cap (default: 20)
# TASKPUMP_DISK_COOLDOWN=   # seconds to wait after a reclaim before another (default: 180)
# TASKPUMP_PANIC_RECLAIM=   # 0 = prune only, never clean build dirs (default: 1)
# TASKPUMP_DISK_WATCHDOG_LOG=  # where TP-PUMP redirects the watchdog it auto-starts
#                           #   (default: <state dir>/.taskpump-disk-watchdog.log). The
#                           #   watchdog itself does not read it; run by hand it logs to
#                           #   its own stdout.
# TASKPUMP_ORIGINAL_CAP=    # the cap to restore once free space recovers (default: the
#                           #   cap file's contents at startup, else
#                           #   TASKPUMP_JOBS_FALLBACK). Nothing in the tree writes it;
#                           #   it is the watchdog's own key, for a caller that starts
#                           #   the watchdog with the cap already lowered.

# ── systemd unit (systemd/taskpump-pump.service) ─────────────────────────────
# Read only by the unit file, which turns them into command-line flags. They are
# set in the unit's environment, not usually in this file.
# TASKPUMP_BIN=             # path to bin/tp
# TASKPUMP_REPO=            # repo root the unit cds into and drives
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_PHASES=     # phase range to drain, e.g. F55..F63  → --phases
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_JOBS=       # concurrent pool cap                  → --jobs
# TASKPUMP_PUMP_CEILING=    # usage ceiling                        → --usage-ceiling

# ── Runtime contract — set by the pump/runner, not by config ─────────────────
# These carry a decision the supervisor already made into the process that acts
# on it. Setting one here does not configure anything; it is overwritten at
# launch. They are listed so the contract is readable in one place.
#
#   TASKPUMP_WORKSPACE_PATH   the workspace this agent was assigned
#   TASKPUMP_REPO_ROOT        the primary checkout, mounted read-only — and, for
#                             runners/local's `list` and `stop`, the workspace whose
#                             fleet is being asked about (issue #40)
#   TASKPUMP_BRANCH           the branch the agent works on
#   TASKPUMP_PHASE            the phase it drains
#   TASKPUMP_TASK_ID          the lead task it is assigned
#   TASKPUMP_BRIEF            host path of the RENDERED brief
#                             (TASKPUMP_BRIEF_TEMPLATE is the configurable one)
#   TASKPUMP_RESUME_NOTE      host path of the RENDERED resume preamble
#                             (TASKPUMP_RESUME_TEMPLATE is the configurable one)
#   TASKPUMP_CONTAINER_NAME   the agent's name (runners/local exports it; the container
#                             runner names the container from TP_CONTAINER_NAME)
#   TASKPUMP_NOW_S            clock override; the test suites' seam
#   TASKPUMP_ENTRYPOINT_TEST_MODE  entrypoint test seam: `plan` resolves and prints,
#                             `preflight` also runs the hook — neither starts a session
